#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:3
msgid "HTTP Responses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:5
msgid ""
"The Response class extends the :doc:`HTTP Message Class "
"</incoming/message>` with methods only appropriate for a server "
"responding to the client that called it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:13
msgid "Working with the Response"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:15
msgid ""
"A Response class is instantiated for you and passed into your "
"controllers. It can be accessed through ``$this->response``. It is the "
"same instance that ``Services::response()`` returns. We call it the "
"global response instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:19
msgid ""
"Many times you will not need to touch the class directly, since "
"CodeIgniter takes care of sending the headers and the body for you. This "
"is great if the page successfully created the content it was asked to. "
"When things go wrong, or you need to send very specific status codes "
"back, or even take advantage of the powerful HTTP caching, it's there for"
" you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:25
msgid "Setting the Output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:27
msgid ""
"When you need to set the output of the script directly, and not rely on "
"CodeIgniter to automatically get it, you do it manually with the "
"``setBody`` method. This is usually used in conjunction with setting the "
"status code of the response:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:33
msgid ""
"The reason phrase ('OK', 'Created', 'Moved Permanently') will be "
"automatically added, but you can add custom reasons as the second "
"parameter of the ``setStatusCode()`` method:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:38
msgid ""
"You can set format an array into either JSON or XML and set the content "
"type header to the appropriate mime with the ``setJSON()`` and "
"``setXML()`` methods. Typically, you will send an array of data to be "
"converted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:44
msgid "Setting Headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:47
msgid "setHeader()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:49
msgid ""
"Often, you will need to set headers to be set for the response. The "
"Response class makes this very simple to do, with the ``setHeader()`` "
"method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:52
msgid ""
"The first parameter is the name of the header. The second parameter is "
"the value, which can be either a string or an array of values that will "
"be combined correctly when sent to the client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:57
msgid ""
"Using these functions instead of using the native PHP functions allows "
"you to ensure that no headers are sent prematurely, causing errors, and "
"makes testing possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:60
msgid ""
"Since v4.6.0, if you set a header using PHP's native ``header()`` "
"function and then use the ``Response`` class to set the same header, the "
"previous one will be overwritten."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:64
msgid ""
"This method just sets headers to the response instance. So, if you create"
" and return another response instance (e.g., if you call "
":php:func:`redirect()`), the headers set here will not be sent "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:69
msgid "appendHeader()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:71
msgid ""
"If the header exists and can have more than one value, you may use the "
"``appendHeader()`` and ``prependHeader()`` methods to add the value to "
"the end or beginning of the values list, respectively. The first "
"parameter is the name of the header, while the second is the value to "
"append or prepend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:78
msgid "removeHeader()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:80
msgid ""
"Headers can be removed from the response with the ``removeHeader()`` "
"method, which takes the header name as the only parameter. This is not "
"case-sensitive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:88
msgid "Redirect"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:90
msgid "If you want to create a redirect, use the :php:func:`redirect()` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:92
msgid ""
"It returns a ``RedirectResponse`` instance. It is a different instance "
"from the global response instance that ``Services::response()`` returns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:95
msgid ""
"If you set Cookies or Response Headers before you call ``redirect()``, "
"they are set to the global response instance, and they are not "
"automatically copied to the ``RedirectResponse`` instance. To send them, "
"you need to call the ``withCookies()`` or ``withHeaders()`` method "
"manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:100
msgid ""
"If you want to redirect, an instance of ``RedirectResponse`` must be "
"returned in a method of the :doc:`Controller <../incoming/controllers>` "
"or the :doc:`Controller Filter <../incoming/filters>`. Note that the "
"``__construct()`` or the ``initController()`` method cannot return any "
"value. If you forget to return ``RedirectResponse``, no redirection will "
"occur."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:107
msgid "Redirect to a URI path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:109
msgid ""
"When you want to pass a URI path (relative to baseURL), use "
"``redirect()->to()``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:114
msgid ""
"If there is a fragment in your URL that you want to remove, you can use "
"the refresh parameter in the method. Like ``return "
"redirect()->to('admin/home', null, 'refresh');``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:119
msgid "Redirect to a Defined Route"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:121
msgid ""
"When you want to pass a :ref:`route name <using-named-routes>` or "
"Controller::method for :ref:`reverse routing <reverse-routing>`, use "
"``redirect()->route()``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:127
msgid ""
"When passing an argument into the function, it is treated as a route name"
" or Controller::method for reverse routing, not a relative/full URI, "
"treating it the same as using ``redirect()->route()``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:135
msgid "Redirect Back"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:137
msgid "When you want to redirect back, use ``redirect()->back()``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:142
msgid ""
"``redirect()->back()`` is not the same as browser \"back\" button. It "
"takes a visitor to \"the last page viewed during the Session\" when the "
"Session is available. If the Session hasn't been loaded, or is otherwise "
"unavailable, then a sanitized version of HTTP_REFERER will be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:147
msgid "Redirect with Cookies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:149
msgid ""
"If you set Cookies before you call ``redirect()``, they are set to the "
"global response instance, and they are not automatically copied to the "
"``RedirectResponse`` instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:153
msgid ""
"To send the Cookies, you need to call the ``withCookies()`` method "
"manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:159
msgid "Redirect with Headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:161
msgid ""
"If you set Response Headers before you call ``redirect()``, they are set "
"to the global response instance, and they are not automatically copied to"
" the ``RedirectResponse`` instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:165
msgid ""
"To send the Headers, you need to call the ``withHeaders()`` method "
"manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:173
msgid "Redirect Status Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:175
msgid ""
"The default HTTP status code for GET requests is 302. However, when using"
" HTTP/1.1 or later, 303 is used for POST/PUT/DELETE requests and 307 for "
"all other requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:178
msgid "You can specify the status code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:183
msgid ""
"Due to a bug, in v4.3.3 or previous versions, the status code of the "
"actual redirect response might be changed even if a status code was "
"specified. See :ref:`ChangeLog v4.3.4 <v434-redirect-status-code>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:187
msgid ""
"If you don't know HTTP status code for redirection, it is recommended to "
"read `Redirections in HTTP <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-"
"US/docs/Web/HTTP/Redirections>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:193
msgid "Force File Download"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:195
msgid ""
"The Response class provides a simple way to send a file to the client, "
"prompting the browser to download the data to your computer. This sets "
"the appropriate headers to make it happen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:198
msgid ""
"The first parameter is the **name you want the downloaded file to be "
"named**, the second parameter is the file data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:201
msgid ""
"If you set the second parameter to null and ``$filename`` is an existing,"
" readable file path, then its content will be read instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:204
msgid ""
"If you set the third parameter to boolean true, then the actual file MIME"
" type (based on the filename extension) will be sent, so that if your "
"browser has a handler for that type - it can use it."
msgstr ""

