#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:221
msgid ""
"The response object MUST be returned for the download to be sent to the "
"client. This allows the response to be passed through all **after** "
"filters before being sent to the client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:227
msgid "Open File in Browser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:229
msgid ""
"Some browsers can display files such as PDF. To tell the browser to "
"display the file instead of saving it, call the "
"``DownloadResponse::inline()`` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:235
msgid "HTTP Caching"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:237
msgid ""
"Built into the HTTP specification are tools help the client (often the "
"web browser) cache the results. Used correctly, this can lead to a huge "
"performance boost to your application because it will tell the client "
"that they don't need to contact the server at all since nothing has "
"changed. And you can't get faster than that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:241
msgid ""
"This is handled through the ``Cache-Control`` and ``ETag`` headers. This "
"guide is not the proper place for a thorough introduction to all of the "
"cache headers power, but you can get a good understanding over at `Google"
" Developers <https://developers.google.com/web/fundamentals/performance"
"/optimizing-content-efficiency/http-caching>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:245
msgid ""
"By default, all response objects sent through CodeIgniter have HTTP "
"caching turned off. The options and exact circumstances are too varied "
"for us to be able to create a good default other than turning it off. "
"It's simple to set the Cache values to what you need, through the "
"``setCache()`` method:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/outgoing/response.rst:251
msgid ""
"The ``$options`` array simply takes an array of key/value pairs that are,"
" with a couple of exceptions, assigned to the ``Cache-Control`` header. "
"You are free to set all of the options exactly as you need for your "
"specific situation. While most of the options are applied to the ``Cache-"
"Control`` header, it intelligently handles the ``etag`` and ``last-"
"modified`` options to their appropriate header."
msgstr ""

